[38;2;124;124;124mWe have $\mathbb{N} = \{0, 1, 2, \ldots\}$ partitioned into $k \ge 2$ disjoint [0m
[38;2;124;124;124msets $J(1), \ldots, J(k)$, each with positive asymptotic density $d(J(p)) > 0$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mLet $\alpha > 0$ be irrational, $e(x) = e^{2\pi i x}$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mWe need to determine whether:[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m$$\lim_{n \to \infty} \frac{1}{n^2} \sum_{p \neq q} \left( \sum_{j \in J(p,n)} [0m
[38;2;124;124;124me(j\alpha) \right) \cdot \overline{\left( \sum_{j \in J(q,n)} e(j\alpha) [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m\right)} = 0.$$[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mLet me denote $S_p(n) = \sum_{j \in J(p,n)} e(j\alpha)$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThe sum is $\sum_{p \neq q} S_p(n) \overline{S_q(n)}$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mNote that $\sum_{p=1}^k S_p(n) = \sum_{j=0}^{n-1} e(j\alpha)$, since the [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m$J(p,n)$ partition $\{0, 1, \ldots, n-1\}$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mLet $T(n) = \sum_{j=0}^{n-1} e(j\alpha)$. This is a geometric series:[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m$$T(n) = \frac{e(n\alpha) - 1}{e(\alpha) - 1}$$[0m
[38;2;124;124;124mwhich is bounded since $\alpha$ is irrational (so $e(\alpha) \neq 1$). Actually [0m[38;2;124;124;124m$|T(n)| \le \frac{2}{|e(\alpha)-1|}$, so $T(n) = O(1)$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mNow, $\sum_{p \neq q} S_p(n) \overline{S_q(n)} = \left|\sum_p S_p(n)\right|^2 - [0m[38;2;124;124;124m\sum_p |S_p(n)|^2 = |T(n)|^2 - \sum_p |S_p(n)|^2$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mWait, let me check: $\sum_{p,q} S_p \overline{S_q} = \sum_p S_p \cdot [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m\overline{\sum_q S_q} = |T(n)|^2$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mAnd $\sum_{p \neq q} S_p \overline{S_q} = |T(n)|^2 - \sum_p |S_p(n)|^2$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mSo we need:[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m$$\frac{1}{n^2}\left(|T(n)|^2 - \sum_p |S_p(n)|^2\right) \to 0.$$[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mSince $|T(n)|^2 = O(1)$, we have $\frac{|T(n)|^2}{n^2} \to 0$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mSo the question reduces to: does $\frac{1}{n^2} \sum_p |S_p(n)|^2 \to 0$?[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mNow $|S_p(n)|^2 = \sum_{j, j' \in J(p,n)} e((j-j')\alpha)$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mSo $\sum_p |S_p(n)|^2 = \sum_p \sum_{j, j' \in J(p,n)} e((j-j')\alpha) = [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m\sum_{j, j' \in \{0,\ldots,n-1\}, j, j' \text{ in same } J(p)} e((j-j')\alpha)$.[0m
[38;2;124;124;124mLet me think about this differently. We need to show $\frac{1}{n^2}\sum_p |[0m
[38;2;124;124;124mS_p(n)|^2 \to 0$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124m$|S_p(n)|^2 = \sum_{j \in J(p,n)} \sum_{j' \in J(p,n)} e((j-j')\alpha)$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThe diagonal terms ($j = j'$) contribute $|J(p,n)|$ each, so total diagonal [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mcontribution is $n$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThe off-diagonal terms: $\sum_{j \neq j' \in J(p,n)} e((j-j')\alpha)$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mSo $\frac{1}{n^2}\sum_p |S_p(n)|^2 = \frac{n}{n^2} + \frac{1}{n^2}\sum_p \sum_{j[0m[38;2;124;124;124m\neq j' \in J(p,n)} e((j-j')\alpha) = \frac{1}{n} + \frac{1}{n^2}\sum_p \sum_{j [0m[38;2;124;124;124m\neq j' \in J(p,n)} e((j-j')\alpha)$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThe $\frac{1}{n} \to 0$, so we need the off-diagonal part to vanish too.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mHmm, but this isn't straightforward. The issue is that the partition is [0m
[38;2;124;124;124marbitrary (just needs positive density), and we need to show the limit is 0 [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mregardless of the partition.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mLet me think about whether this is actually true. Consider a potential [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mcounterexample.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mLet $k = 2$. Let $J(1)$ be the set of even numbers, $J(2)$ the odd numbers. Both[0m[38;2;124;124;124mhave density $1/2$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124m$S_1(n) = \sum_{j=0, j \text{ even}}^{n-1} e(j\alpha) = \sum_{m=0}[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m^{\lfloor(n-1)/2\rfloor} e(2m\alpha)$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThis is a geometric series with ratio $e(2\alpha)$. Since $2\alpha$ is [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mirrational, $e(2\alpha) \neq 1$, so $|S_1(n)| = O(1)$. Similarly $|S_2(n)| = [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mO(1)$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mSo $\sum_p |S_p(n)|^2 = O(1)$, and $\frac{1}{n^2} \sum_p |S_p(n)|^2 \to 0$. [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mGood, limit is 0 in this case.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mNow consider a more adversarial partition. The key question: can we construct a [0m[38;2;124;124;124mpartition with positive densities where $|S_p(n)|$ grows linearly in $n$?[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mFor $|S_p(n)|$ to be large (order $n$), we'd need the terms $e(j\alpha)$ for $j [0m[38;2;124;124;124m\in J(p,n)$ to be highly correlated/aligned. But since $\alpha$ is irrational, [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m$e(j\alpha)$ is equidistributed on the unit circle. So for a "generic" set [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m$J(p)$, the sum should be small.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mBut the partition is arbitrary. Can we choose $J(p)$ to pick out terms where [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m$e(j\alpha)$ is close to some fixed direction?[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mFor example, let $J(1) = \{j : \text{Re}(e(j\alpha)) > 0\}$ and $J(2) = \{j : [0m
